          "text": "# Tools Documentation - AG-UI Protocol\n\n## Overview\nTools in AG-UI are functions enabling AI agents to interact with external systems and incorporate human judgment. They bridge AI reasoning with real-world actions, allowing developers to create sophisticated human-in-the-loop experiences.\n\n## Core Functionality\nTools allow agents to:\n- Request specific information\n- Perform actions in external systems\n- Ask for human input or confirmation\n- Access specialized capabilities\n\n## Tool Structure\nEach tool follows a consistent format with:\n- **Name**: Unique identifier\n- **Description**: Explanation of purpose\n- **Parameters**: JSON Schema defining accepted arguments\n\nAs stated: \"The `parameters` field uses JSON Schema to define the structure of arguments that the tool accepts.\"\n\n## Key Architectural Advantage\nFrontend-defined tools offer important benefits: \"The frontend determines what capabilities are available to the agent\" and tools can dynamically adjust based on user permissions and application state.\n\n## Tool Call Lifecycle\nThe system follows three phases:\n1. **ToolCallStart**: Initiates the call with unique ID\n2. **ToolCallArgs**: Streams arguments as JSON fragments\n3. **ToolCallEnd**: Marks completion\n\n## Human-in-the-Loop Integration\nTools enable workflows where \"AI suggests actions that require human approval\" and support \"collaborative decision-making\" between agents and users.\n\n## Best Practices\nEffective tool design requires clear naming, detailed descriptions, structured parameters, and robust error handling for optimal agent performance.",
